Many core Internet protocols, such as DNS, SMTP, POP3, IMAP and Telnet, were designed without security in mind, so their traffic travels in cleartext and can be read, forged or altered by anyone on the path. A family of newer protocols adds authenticity, integrity and confidentiality on top of, or in place of, these older designs. Every protocol belongs to specific layers of the OSI or TCP/IP model and behaves according to their functions, and protocols built on TCP or UDP run on specific ports; most secure versions keep the original workflow, wrap it in encryption and move it to a port of its own.
DNS (Domain Name System) resolves domain names to IP addresses, letting people remember example.com instead of 93.184.216.34. A host sends a DNS query, for example for an A record (IPv4) or an AAAA record (IPv6); in a tshark port 53 capture, host 192.168.0.102 asks server 1.0.0.1 about example.com and receives 93.184.216.34 and 2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946. The problem is that anyone on the network can send a forged response, and the querying host accepts any answer, which could lead it to a rogue server. DNSSEC prevents this by having the zone owner sign all DNS records with its private key and publish its public key so users can verify the signatures. Signed records provide authenticity, because the record is signed with the owner's private key and therefore comes from that owner, and integrity, because any change in transit invalidates the signature.
Email faces similar problems. When the first email was sent in 1971 the security landscape was very different, and SMTP (sending and server-to-server delivery), POP3 (retrieving mail) and IMAP (synchronising a mailbox with the server) all work in cleartext, making an email like a postcard that any intruder can read. Webmail first ran over HTTP, between the browser and a web server that used a mail server to send and receive messages, so the same confidentiality and integrity issues remained. Once HTTPS became the standard, traffic between browser and web server was encrypted, but the web and mail servers could still read messages, and mail could still cross the Internet in cleartext over SMTP. SSL/TLS was eventually added to all email protocols, turning SMTP, POP3 and IMAP into SMTPS, POP3S and IMAPS, where the "S" means secure. Mail is then encrypted between client and server, yet the mail servers can still read it and must be trusted. Users who do not want to trust them can use OpenPGP, an open standard for signing and encrypting files and email described in RFC 4880, which grew out of PGP (Pretty Good Privacy), created by Phil Zimmermann. GnuPG (GPG) is a free, open-source implementation that integrates with mail clients so that only the intended recipient, not even the mail servers, can read a message. Each user creates a key pair with gpg --gen-key, entering a name and email address, keeps the private key secure and shares the public key. The sender signs with their own private key and encrypts with the recipient's public key; the recipient checks the signature with the sender's public key and decrypts with their own private key. On the command line this is gpg --encrypt --sign --armor -r strategos@tryhackme.thm message.txt, where --encrypt -r encrypts with the recipient's public key for confidentiality, --sign signs with the sender's private key for authenticity, and --armor produces ASCII instead of binary output. The result, message.txt.asc, begins with -----BEGIN PGP MESSAGE----- and can be sent with any email client. OpenPGP protects the confidentiality and integrity of the message contents, but not the email headers.
Two of the secure mail protocols deserve a closer look. SMTPS is SMTP wrapped in TLS, providing authentication, integrity and confidentiality. SMTP is an email push protocol that moves mail from a client to a server, whereas POP3 downloads mail from the server to the client. SMTP works in two models: end-to-end, used between organizations, where the sender's SMTP client connects directly to the recipient's SMTP server, and store-and-forward, used within an organization, where the server keeps a copy of the message until it forwards it. The User Agent (UA) creates the message and passes it to the Mail Transfer Agent (MTA), which carries it across the Internet to the recipient's MTA; the MTA and the Mail Delivery Agent (MDA) are often on the same server. Encryption is switched on with the STARTTLS command, and SMTPS commonly uses ports 587 and 465. It protects against sniffing and is also said to hinder spam from compromised domains, data exfiltration and phishing. POP3S is the secure version of POP3, which retrieves mail from the MDA to the Mail User Agent (MUA): the client connects to the server, downloads all queued messages or only selected ones, stores them on the device that connected, and by default the server deletes its copy. POP3 offers no synchronization across devices and sends credentials and messages in cleartext, exposing them to sniffing; POP3S wraps the session in TLS via STARTTLS and uses port 995 instead of POP3's port 110.
Remote administration followed the same path. Networked systems created the need to log in and run processes remotely, and the earliest protocols, Telnet and remote login (clients telnet and rlogin), protected neither the confidentiality of traffic, including credentials, nor the integrity of commands, so an attacker could read logins and modify commands. A Wireshark "Follow TCP Stream" on a Telnet session shows the username michael and the password RJ9wn^t3T%gC in cleartext, readable by any well-placed packet capture even though the password is strong by modern standards (client text appears in red, server text in blue). The Secure Shell Protocol (SSH) supplies the missing security: an attacker can no longer feasibly read credentials or modify traffic, and following an SSH stream reveals only version numbers and supported protocols.
Secure Socket Layer (SSL) and Transport Layer Security (TLS) encrypt data between a client, such as a browser, and a server. TLS acts as a wrapper around other protocols, turning HTTP into HTTPS on port 443 and FTP into FTPS on port 990, and it has gradually replaced SSL, which is rarely used today. Its handshake has seven steps. In the Client Hello, the client sends its TLS version, supported cipher suites and random bytes. In the Server Hello, the server replies with its certificate, the chosen cipher suite and its own random bytes. The client then authenticates the certificate through the certificate authority that issued it, as browsers come pre-installed with CA certificates and can, for example, verify Google's certificate. Next, the client encrypts random bytes, the premaster secret, with the server's public key taken from the certificate, and the server decrypts it with its private key. Both sides then generate the same session key from the client random, the server random and the premaster secret, without ever transmitting it. Finally, each side sends a "finished" message encrypted with the session key, and encrypted communication begins.
HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) is the encrypted version of HTTP, the client-server protocol that carries data between a web browser and a website. Plain HTTP sends everything in cleartext over TCP port 80: a user agent, such as a browser or an application using an API, sends requests to a web server, where GET asks for a resource such as a page, an image or a file, and POST and PUT send data to the server. Anyone who captures the packets can read them, which may be acceptable for reading about a company's products but not for entering a password or a credit card number. HTTPS adds SSL/TLS on top of HTTP and uses port 443 by default. TLS combines asymmetric encryption, with a public and a private key, to negotiate a symmetric algorithm and a shared secret key, and symmetric encryption to protect the actual traffic, so intercepted packets can be neither read nor altered, preserving confidentiality and integrity. Without HTTPS, online banking and online payments would not be possible.
FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is much older, dating back to 1971 and RFC 114 and later refined in RFC 765 and RFC 959. It follows the client-server model and authenticates users with a username and password, but both the login and the files travel unencrypted. It uses two channels: a control connection on TCP port 21 for commands and authentication, and a data connection for transferring files and folders. In active mode, the client opens the control connection and the server connects back to the client from port 20 to send data, which fails when the client is behind a firewall that blocks incoming connections. In passive mode, the client sends the PASV command, the server replies with a random port and the client opens the data connection itself, which works well behind firewalls. FTP supports several data types: ASCII (Type A) for text, Image (Type I) for binary byte-by-byte transfer, EBCDIC (Type E) for that character set, and Local (Type L n) for systems without 8-bit bytes. FTPS adds TLS to both the command and data channels in one of two ways. Implicit FTPS encrypts the connection automatically from the start and uses port 990, while explicit FTPS connects on port 21 and explicitly asks the server to start a TLS session, after which the client can encrypt only the control channel, only the data channel, or both. Either way, FTPS protects login credentials and transferred files from sniffing attacks.
Socket Secure (SOCKS) is a proxy protocol that exchanges data through a delegate server, the SOCKS5 proxy, and is used to secure application-layer protocols; the Squid server, for example, implements SOCKS5 to transfer data over HTTP. If client A wants to reach client B but a firewall stands between them, A connects to the proxy and sends 0x05 (SOCKS version 5), then 0x01 (authentication is supported), then one or more bytes (0x00, 0x01, 0x02 or 0x03, of variable length) listing its supported authentication methods. The proxy answers with the method it chose, A sends a request packet containing BHOST and BPORT, and a session between A and the proxy is established; B associates with the proxy the same way, after which both exchange data routed through the proxy. SOCKS5 hides internal details from routing over the Internet and, acting as a relay, can bypass censorship based on the client's IP address.
Internet Protocol Security (IPsec, here IPsec-v3) adds authentication, integrity and confidentiality at the IP level using three protocols. The Authentication Header (AH) provides authentication and integrity but not confidentiality, suiting cases where those two are enough; it is optional in IPsec-v3 but was mandatory to implement in IPsec-v2. The Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) adds encryption to authentication and integrity. The Security Association (SA) negotiates encryption keys and algorithms, with Internet Key Exchange (IKE) as one example. Both AH and ESP work in two modes: transport mode protects the TCP/UDP header and data, while tunnel mode also covers the IP header, with AH providing authentication and ESP providing confidentiality and integrity.
Finally, TCP/IP was designed for availability rather than confidentiality or integrity, since the Internet was meant to withstand a nuclear attack, which is why routing protocols adapt quickly when a link fails. Companies still need to connect offices securely over this infrastructure, and a Virtual Private Network (VPN) does so by creating a private, secure connection over a public, insecure network, linking, for instance, a remote office and a remote user to the main office. A VPN needs a VPN client and a VPN server or concentrator, and all traffic between them is encrypted. The two most common VPN protocols are IPsec and SSL/TLS. IPsec's ESP is ideal for tunnels between two networks or between a computer and a network, securing all data between the endpoints and, in tunnel mode, even hiding the IP address when the system is behind a VPN concentrator; Cisco VPN systems use IPsec. Although SSL was created to secure HTTP, OpenVPN uses SSL/TLS and the tools and libraries built around TLS to offer various authentication and encryption mechanisms. Some older VPN protocols are no longer secure, Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol (PPTP) being one example.
